#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120918} :activity/id #uuid "c5afec26-f914-51c6-81d2-b03d5e027b77" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048648 :topic/id #uuid "b1e9fe5c-895e-5616-8f6f-1ceb3b387545" :topic/math-academy-id 1836} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120918 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121136 :tutorial/content "Recall that if the curve $\\mathbf r(s)$ is parametrized by arc length $s,$ then the curvature of $\\mathbf{r}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\kappa(s) =  \\left\\| \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf T}{\\textrm{d}s}  \\right\\|.\n$$\n\nMoreover, if $\\mathbf r(t)$ is parametrized by a general parameter $t,$ we have the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf T(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|}$ is the unit tangent vector to the curve. \n\nIt can be shown that the curvature of $\\mathbf r(t)$ can also be found using the following cross product formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this formula at the end of the lesson. For now, let's get some practice at applying it." :tutorial/id #uuid "699ec19c-b03f-5cc8-8096-613245aa4961" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7883 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3e7c32fc-b2a5-5b01-be28-7f715f065fb1" :step/math-academy-id 45439 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120919}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120919 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119947 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119517 :question/id #uuid "f0213e01-520f-5b59-96d3-1d94a50deb82" :question/math-academy-id "e-12591"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eddc00c0-eac2-5e79-84be-b90cc27eb054" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049196}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444333}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "182c4b73-5dc9-53a4-835c-6d143dc732a6" :step/math-academy-id 45443 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120923}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120920 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121137 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a plane curve $C$ that is parametrized by the vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\langle x(t), \\: y(t) \\rangle.\n$$\n\nAt first glance, it looks as though the cross product curvature formula cannot be applied since cross products apply to three-dimensional vectors, whereas the given vector $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ has only two dimensions.\n\nHowever, this curve can also be viewed as a curve in three-dimensional space that lies on the plane $z=0.$ In this case, $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ can be considered as a vector-valued function with $z$-component equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)= \\langle x(t), \\: y(t), \\: 0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nAs a result, the curvature $\\kappa$ of our plane curve $C$ can be calculated using our cross product formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t)=\\dfrac{\\|{\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t)}\\times {\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime\\prime}(t)}\\|}{\\|{\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t)}\\|^3}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2ae1ba4c-311d-5298-8526-146a426bda14" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7884 :tutorial/title "Applying the Cross Product Formula to Plane Curves"} :step/id #uuid "405daa49-6e22-58fb-86e9-6e879a856c32" :step/math-academy-id 45446 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120921}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120921 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119948 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119518 :question/id #uuid "b17659af-7eb8-5922-a8b7-ab1e9dcfda08" :question/math-academy-id "e-12592"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca07aabe-9fdd-5f28-b39f-1971c3591f86" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484263}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve"} :step/id #uuid "bf0440fe-1ba1-52ce-be45-c99625d9108a" :step/math-academy-id 45447 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120922}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120922 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121138 :tutorial/content "Recall that if the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ is parametrized by a general parameter $t,$ then the curvature of $\\mathbf{r}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf T(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|}$ is the unit tangent vector to the curve. \n\nHere, $\\mathbf r'(t)$ is a vector function and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|$ is a scalar function.\nSo, we differentiate $\\mathbf T(t)$ by using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(T) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖})\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)+\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({r}^{′}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖})\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)+\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\\cdot {r}^{″}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the cross product of a vector with itself equals $\\mathbf{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t)\\times {T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\\times (\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖})\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)+\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\\cdot {r}^{″}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{2}}\\,({r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the magnitude of both sides and use the fact that $\\mathbf T$ is a unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖T(t)\\times {T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{2}}\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ \\\\ ‖T(t)‖\\cdot ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{2}}\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ \\\\ 1\\cdot ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{2}}\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|} = \\dfrac{\\left\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\right\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f59936dc-ef8e-53d7-be8e-244d577dd658" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7885 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Cross Product Formula"} :step/id #uuid "1ca0f550-1726-5b03-9c0f-4d507624654b" :step/math-academy-id 45450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120923 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119949 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119519 :question/id #uuid "0ad622cf-852f-5795-81ec-d147f050074e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12608"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b61ddc7-b781-5a25-b499-1ea6f48abf36" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444330}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a General Point"} :step/id #uuid "390940d4-be4b-5226-9968-df737ec5e680" :step/math-academy-id 45507 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120920}}] :activity/title "Finding Curvature Using the Cross Product" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120035}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484328} :activity/id #uuid "f88d5adc-1895-5ddf-9de6-45e58f509300" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048648 :topic/id #uuid "b1e9fe5c-895e-5616-8f6f-1ceb3b387545" :topic/math-academy-id 1836} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484304 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354511 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1531abda-6b9e-57a0-85f8-a314eedab0cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351753} {:answer/id #uuid "88309aca-4d50-5129-b6ef-7913f78d97f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352760} {:answer/id #uuid "89c7f87a-5e05-5ea2-9af4-aa43698ddf5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t))^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352769} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbd59e6-4ef2-5b7d-8bb7-23097c14bac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353214} {:answer/id #uuid "db666671-7f53-5434-afee-7634c681014c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t))^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353473}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352769} :answer-field/id #uuid "54ee2fe4-94d2-55d2-8a33-2d8eb87dbae3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bcaa2ffa-aada-5b80-a745-a2dbdb67535d" :question/math-academy-id "q-86799" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨-t,\\,4,\\,1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le \\pi $, use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature as a function of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩=⟨-1,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative ${r}^{″}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross-product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨-1,0,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\times ⟨0,0,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\ge 0$ when $0\\le t\\le \\pi .$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t))^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0d33b287-b538-55b3-b3c7-cf0142e26701" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484347}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484328 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "012ce73b-ccad-5736-8a98-79181d781399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{t(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315120} {:answer/id #uuid "2b42cc41-9300-5600-adab-14f258488843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{t(4+9{t}^{2})^{1/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315371} {:answer/id #uuid "7903666a-0bc5-545c-8cca-acb01e2c736b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6t}{(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315796} {:answer/id #uuid "c16bf73b-f3b4-5ef9-bf8c-52d15609bd27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{t(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316204} {:answer/id #uuid "c85c337a-02ca-5277-ab4f-284e2592cc3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6{t}^{2}}{(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316248}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316204} :answer-field/id #uuid "517a7074-69ee-57e9-839f-2627066da920" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a8320061-9385-5e97-8a78-62a775ef29e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-34381" :question/problem "What is the curvature of the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},{t}^{3}⟩$, for $t>0?$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩=⟨2t,3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(3{t}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =t\\sqrt{4+9{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $t>0$, we have $|t|=t.$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩=⟨2,6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨2t,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,0⟩\\times ⟨2,\\,6t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & 3{t}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}2t & 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 2 & 6t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖=6{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}}{(t\\sqrt{4+9{t}^{2}})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}}{{t}^{3}(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{t(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "723416d5-8258-5723-a84d-1b6f2871325d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484334}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484334 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368805 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2477f3d1-be5e-5e0e-bc3f-f8f5b8edbca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368807} {:answer/id #uuid "973685dc-4766-5b17-a27c-c29e7950b014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\,t}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368808} {:answer/id #uuid "db01273c-05b0-5ec6-878b-daa8f81112b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}\\,t}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368809} {:answer/id #uuid "b40c4ad9-89bc-5751-92eb-9420503c5bda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368810} {:answer/id #uuid "9a666d0e-c4fc-52b8-b6f8-071b434f357f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368811}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368807} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cce1805-1c9a-5fca-a977-0fbb55ad4d80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eff11497-0bb2-5558-843d-3319ac120d5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-83403" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4t⟩,$ use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t.$ \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j-9k.$*" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are told that\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j-9k,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-9)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144+81} \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{{5}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8e9f96eb-3033-5b55-bf1d-bd63b264150d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484304}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484347 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0421500e-2616-57c8-9da2-689ccd40356c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483575} {:answer/id #uuid "668782ba-0b98-5b7f-b25e-71892937ec4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483764} {:answer/id #uuid "6e3f626c-c384-5ec1-b671-ad33ea606c0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483783} {:answer/id #uuid "7d137241-98f2-54be-bd88-0f9e32e0f2bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483820} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6d79a5-1689-5b95-8ee8-41606efa235f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483912}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483575} :answer-field/id #uuid "795c37b4-5f92-5e3b-aa83-2f9b29aa6f03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "92ddc7e5-b6d3-5672-8ade-2787d57915e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-132014" :question/problem "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $r(t)=⟨t,\\,{e}^{-t}⟩$ as a function of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-{e}^{-t})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{-2t}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}{{e}^{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,{e}^{-t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨1,\\,-{e}^{-t},\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,{e}^{-t},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t} & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}{{e}^{t}})}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{3}}}{{e}^{3t}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bdfbf0ab-5e71-5eab-a28e-ae335d919cc6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484361}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484361 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26d1ccff-7494-5f51-adcb-7caa359b5d22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{{(4{t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483634} {:answer/id #uuid "6528173f-e8b1-5a42-8e0f-015496528630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{(4{t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483760} {:answer/id #uuid "abbefdf6-1ec8-5f22-84de-6723b7e1d7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{(4{t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483913} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c19760-4ded-5e81-b3c9-2b434625b1c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{(4{t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483934} {:answer/id #uuid "f20d2290-e591-52af-92b9-bd4181fb6027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484051}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483760} :answer-field/id #uuid "32a2621c-52bf-54ef-b979-b5a1bacfe6d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a7e2dc04-d285-5815-98e3-c99d4176897c" :question/math-academy-id "q-132051" :question/problem "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $r(t)=⟨4{t}^{2},\\,4t⟩$ as a function of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =4⟨2t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =4\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =4⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =4⟨2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =8⟨1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =(4⟨2t,\\,1,\\,0⟩)\\times (8⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩) \\\\  & =32\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =32\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}2t & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-32\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖=32.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{(4\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1})}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{64\\sqrt{{(4{t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{(4{t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ec4b6043-94ea-5557-8d3d-cc725d5c7999"}] :activity/title "Finding Curvature Using the Cross Product" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483566}]}
